## N4h — THE DERIVATION PATH, WITH ITS DEAD ENDS

**Dead end one — the flat-only conjecture itself.** The supervising record
announced the extension before the work was done: *literal gluing plus
positivity admits exactly the flat cell*. It is refuted by `W1`, a matrix with
eight positive rational minors, at the **all-plus** class and at magnitude
`1/4`, where nothing about the geometry is strained. That is correction `111`,
disclosed rather than smoothed, and `break_witness_w1` asserts the un-corrected
reading. **The block that named the branch open was right not to guess it.**

**Dead end two — expecting a non-isotropic branch to classify.** The natural
worry was that the sixteen relations might carry a second, messier component
which the semialgebraic argument would have to handle separately. Chasing it
would have been weeks. The fix was to stop looking for branches and **prove the
converse**: the polynomial normal form kills all sixteen identically, so there
is nothing else on the domain. A search became a two-line certificate.

**Dead end three — treating the sixty-four sign cells as sixty-four branches.**
The first pass classified per sign pattern and produced sixty-four regions with
a suspicious amount of repetition. The repetition was the signal: the
corner-sign congruence `D -> E D E` preserves the form family, symmetry and
positive definiteness, and its orbits are four classes of sixteen. **Sixty-four
regions collapsed to two independent labels**, and the individual signs went
where they belonged, into the convention fence.

**Dead end four — searching for a positive point at the landed magnitudes.**
Block 209 had proved `(3/5, 4/5)` indefinite and the instinct was to look
*elsewhere* on the magnitude base. The right question was which **class** the
landed point sat in. Flipping one sign per offset moves it to `(-1, -1)`, where
the whole base is admissible, and `W3` is positive at the identical magnitudes
and volumes. **The counterexample was never about how curved the cell was.**

**Dead end five — reading "classification" as a statement about individual
matrices.** At a fixed admissible moduli point the solution still carries four
duality coordinates, and only an open bounded subset of them is positive. The
independent check caught the overreach; the fix was to measure the region — an
interior choice that works, `a = 1` and `b = 2` that do not — and to say
**PD-solvable** everywhere the headline says anything at all.

**What actually worked.** Noticing that the coefficient matrix is constant, so
one structural fact governs every moduli point at once; counting the divisions
instead of trusting them; proving the converse rather than enumerating branches;
letting a congruence group collapse the sign labels; promoting Block 209's
parameter-freedom argument from a point to symbolic moduli, which is the step
that turns a no-go into a region; and reconciling every landed number **through
the parent runner's own literals** rather than retyping them.

